## TODO: pak-entries-schema

Phase 2, only after pak-analyzer is verified. Work happens in apps/index/, on the
PRODUCTION Postgres path. The legacy build-index.ts gets nothing.

REWRITTEN 2026-08-01: the original text targeted a single index.db written by
build-index.ts. The schema now lives in Postgres and reaches users as per-game SQLite
snapshots, so this is two schema changes (source of truth, then export), not one.

- Postgres table in postgres/schema.ts, added as a new idempotent migration:
  pak_entries(sha256 REFERENCES file_contents, entry_path, entry_hash, entry_size)
  plus a per-sha256 manifest status (ok / encrypted / no-directory-index), keyed on
  CONTENT hash so identical paks shipped by multiple mods store one manifest.
- Export side, postgres/export-sqlite.ts: the snapshot is built by explicit SELECTs into
  an explicit SQLite schema, so pak_entries does NOT reach the app until the exporter is
  taught to carry it. This is the step the old single-DB plan did not have, and it is
  also the natural size lever (see manifest-backfill-and-size): the exporter can carry
  manifests for some games and not others.
- Safety constraint that drove the design, re-verified 2026-08-01 against
  src-tauri/src/commands/mod_index.rs: all five queries name their columns explicitly and
  open read-only, so NEW tables and columns are invisible to shipped apps - safe. What is
  NOT safe is adding rows to the mods table for mods without indexed files: query_by_name
  identifies a mod only when exactly one LIKE match exists, so bloating mods would
  silently break name identification. pak_entries must not touch existing tables.
  The frozen legacy index.db is a separate producer and is unaffected either way.
- Wire the parser into postgres/process-content.ts, at the point where it already calls
  extractContentEntries from content-archive.ts: the pak bytes are in memory there when
  the SHA256 is computed, so new mods get manifests with zero extra downloads or API
  calls. Manifests attach to file_contents rows, so the INSERT sits alongside the
  existing jsonb_to_recordset insert rather than replacing it.
- Backfill for the already-indexed PD3/Crime Boss pak files (they need one re-download to
  parse their indexes): drive it off a "file_contents rows with no manifest status" query
  rather than the legacy builder's getFileIdsMissingNames/--backfill mechanism, which
  belongs to build-index.ts and does not exist on the Postgres path. Restrict the set to
  PD3 and Crime Boss: PD2/PDTH/RAID marker files are not paks and are excluded from
  manifests entirely.
- Re-measure the affected file count before running anything. The old estimate of ~7,500
  pak files out of ~29k mods predates RAID support and months of indexing, so treat it as
  a stale number, not a plan input.

